<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Contemplative Tradition" cic="CCC 2700-2724" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What are the three expressions of prayer according to the Catechism? Briefly describe each one.</li>
<li>Why does the Catechism say contemplation is a gift and not a technique? (CCC 2713)</li>
<li>Name and explain the four steps of lectio divina.</li>
<li>Who is called to contemplative prayer? Only monks, or all the baptized? (CCC 2714)</li>
<li>What is the &laquo;dark night&raquo; according to St. John of the Cross, and how does it differ from a crisis of faith?</li>
<li>In the scene of Martha and Mary (Lk 10:38-42), why does Jesus say Mary &laquo;has chosen the good portion&raquo;?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no &laquo;right&raquo; answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. St. Teresa struggled for eighteen years with prayer before it blossomed. At what stage of your prayer life are you? Do you feel more like Teresa the distracted or Teresa the one in love with God?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be honest. God doesn't need you to fake fervor. The struggle is normal.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Jesus said: &laquo;When you pray, go into your room, close the door, and pray to your Father who is in secret&raquo; (Mt 6:6). Do you have an &laquo;inner room&raquo; — a time and place to be alone with God? If not, how could you create one?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">It doesn't have to be a chapel. It could be a corner of your room, a park bench, your commute.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Do you identify more with Martha (doing, doing, doing) or Mary (sitting and listening)? Do you think your life has a balance between action and contemplation?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Few people are 100% Martha or 100% Mary. Most of us lean more toward one. Which way does your scale tip?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Have you ever experienced &laquo;dryness&raquo; in prayer — feeling nothing, sensing that God is not listening? How did you react?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">If you drifted away from prayer because of it, that's okay. Now you know dryness is a normal part of the journey. Could you try again with this new understanding?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Charles de Foucauld taught the &laquo;practice of the presence of God&raquo;: saying from time to time: &laquo;God is here, now, with me.&raquo; At what moments of your day could you introduce this practice? What would change if you did it for a week?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be concrete: on waking up, before each meal, leaving home, going to bed. Pick three moments.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Guided Lectio Divina &mdash; Psalm 131</h4>
<p>This week you will do a complete lectio divina with Psalm 131. Find 15 minutes in a quiet place. Follow these steps and write what you discover:</p>

<p><strong>Step 1 &mdash; Lectio (Read):</strong> Read Psalm 131 very slowly, three times. Aloud softly or silently. Underline or copy here the word or phrase that strikes you most:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Step 2 &mdash; Meditatio (Meditate):</strong> Ask yourself: What does God want to say to me <em>today</em> through this phrase? What aspect of my life does it touch? What does it invite me to change? Repeat the phrase mentally and write your reflections:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Step 3 &mdash; Oratio (Pray):</strong> Now respond to God in your own words. You can write a brief prayer of thanksgiving, petition, or silence:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Step 4 &mdash; Contemplatio (Contemplate):</strong> Stay in silence for three minutes. Without writing. Without thinking about the text. Just be with God. If distractions come, let them pass. Afterwards, write one word that sums up what you experienced:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Practice of the Presence of God</h4>
<p>For three days, set silent alarms on your phone three times a day. When they go off, stop for an instant and say inwardly: &laquo;God is here, now, with me.&raquo; Don't interrupt what you're doing. Just acknowledge. At the end of the third day, write whether you noticed any change (even a small one):</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. The Silence Challenge</h4>
<p>Pick one day this week. On that day, every time you feel the impulse to pick up your phone unnecessarily (boredom, habit), don't do it. Instead, close your eyes for ten seconds and breathe slowly. At the end of the day, write how you felt:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Scripture to Memorize</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>"O LORD, my heart is not lifted up, my eyes are not raised too high; I do not occupy myself with things too great and too marvelous for me. But I have calmed and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with its mother."</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; Psalm 131:1-2</p>
</div>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>"When you pray, go into your room, close the door, and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees in secret will reward you."</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; Matthew 6:6</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord, You who let Yourself be found in the gentle whisper,<br />
teach me to listen for You in the silence.<br />
In a world that shouts, give me attentive ears.<br />
In my restless heart, plant peace.<br />
Do not let me be only Martha — running without a soul —,<br />
but give me Mary's courage to sit at Your feet.<br />
May my prayer not be empty words,<br />
but a loving presence.<br />
And may I, in gazing at You, learn to gaze at my brothers and sisters<br />
with the same tenderness with which You gaze at me.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

